        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
          1. Gor'kov Acoustic Potential & Primary Radiation Force (F_rad)
        </h4>
        <p>
          In a high-frequency acoustic standing wave, dispersed particles experience an acoustic radiation force derived from the spatial gradient of the time-averaged Gor'kov acoustic potential field $U$:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-xl border border-hairline font-mono text-[11px] text-ink overflow-x-auto">
          F_rad = - ∇U = - (π · P0² · V_p · β_m / (2 · λ)) · Φ(β, ρ) · sin(2 · k · x)
        </div>
        <p>
          where P_0 is the acoustic pressure amplitude, V_p is particle volume, β_m is the matrix compressibility, λ is acoustic wavelength, and Φ(β, ρ) represents the non-dimensional acoustic contrast factor:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-xl border border-hairline font-mono text-[11px] text-ink overflow-x-auto">
          Φ(β, ρ) = [ (5·ρ_p - 2·ρ_m) / (2·ρ_p + ρ_m) ] - [ β_p / β_m ]
        </div>
        <p>
          For lipid droplets and milk fat globules (ρ_p ≈ 915 kg/m³, β_p ≈ 5.21 × 10⁻¹⁰ Pa⁻¹), Φ &lt; 0 (negative contrast). As a consequence, fat globules are driven away from acoustic pressure nodes toward pressure <strong>antinodes</strong>. Conversely, dense microalgal biomass and cellular debris (Φ &gt; 0) concentrate at pressure <strong>nodes</strong>, enabling seamless, simultaneous multi-phase fractionation without centrifugation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <div class="space-y-3 bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-2xl border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-600"></span>
          2. Secondary Bjerknes Forces & Accelerated Creaming
        </h4>
        <p>
          While the primary Gor'kov force brings droplets into localized nodal planes, inter-particle interactions are governed by secondary acoustic radiation forces—known as <strong>Bjerknes forces</strong> (F_Bjerknes). When two adjacent globules oscillate under the same acoustic frequency:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-xl border border-hairline font-mono text-[11px] text-ink overflow-x-auto">
          F_Bjerknes = - (2 · π · ρ_m / d²) · &lang; V̇_1 · V̇_2 &rang; · [ (3 · cos²θ - 1) ]
        </div>
        <p>
          When globules align perpendicular to the acoustic wave propagation axis, the secondary Bjerknes force is strongly attractive. Droplets rapidly coalesce into stable multi-globule aggregates. According to Stokes' Law, the buoyant creaming velocity scales with the square of the effective cluster diameter (v_cream ∝ D_eff²). Clustering increases the effective diameter from 3.8 µm to over 85 µm, accelerating gravitational phase separation by over <strong>450-fold</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <div class="space-y-3 bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-2xl border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
          3. Dual-Frequency Synergy & Acoustic Streaming Suppression
        </h4>
        <p>
          A single high-frequency field generates Rayleigh and Schlichting boundary acoustic streaming eddies that can destabilize particle bands. By coupling a primary 400 kHz low-megasonic standing wave (wide node spacing λ1 / 2 ≈ 1.88 mm, high trapping depth) with an orthogonal 1.6 MHz high-megasonic array (λ2 / 2 ≈ 0.47 mm), the dual-frequency system creates:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-muted">
          <li><strong>Hierarchical Trapping:</strong> 400 kHz captures macro-globules (&gt; 3 µm) while 1.6 MHz captures submicron colloids and haze.</li>
          <li><strong>Eddy Phase-Damping:</strong> The dual standing waves disrupt continuous convective recirculating loops, maintaining a tranquil laminar boundary.</li>
          <li><strong>Cavitation Immunity:</strong> Because cavitation inception pressure scales exponentially above 400 kHz (P_Blake &gt; 1.85 MPa), the fluid remains strictly cavitation-free with zero free-radical (•OH) formation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <div class="space-y-3 bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-2xl border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-600"></span>
          4. 3-A Sanitary Design & Zero-Shear Bioactive Preservation
        </h4>
        <p>
          Conventional disc-stack centrifuges subject milk fat globules to extreme mechanical shear stress (&gt; 15,000 Pa), stripping delicate xanthine oxidase, glycoproteins, and sphingomyelin from the Native Milk Fat Globule Membrane (MFGM). This releases free fatty acids, inducing hydrolytic rancidity.
        </p>
        <p>
          The acoustophoretic flow-through chamber operates under ultra-low hydrodynamic shear (&lt; 0.05 Pa), achieving <strong>100.0% retention of native MFGM polar lipids</strong>. Built to 3-A Sanitary Standard 63-04 and EHEDG Class I aseptic specifications, the electropolished 316L stainless steel chamber (Ra &lt; 0.4 µm) features full Clean-In-Place (CIP) and Steam-In-Place (SIP at 130°C) compatibility with zero internal dead-legs.
        </p>
